      "The ancestors of the Inuit, the Thule people, migrated eastward from Alaska across the Canadian Arctic and into Greenland beginning around 1000–1200 CE, bringing with them advanced marine mammal hunting technology (especially bowhead whale hunting), dog sleds, and umiak (large skin boats). They displaced or absorbed the earlier Dorset culture people, whose spiritual practices may have influenced Thule-Inuit traditions, though evidence is limited. By the time of European contact, Inuit societies were distributed across the vast Arctic, organized into regional groups with distinct dialects, seasonal migration patterns, and cultural variations, but sharing core cosmological beliefs, hunting practices, and social organization based on extended family camps and seasonal gatherings.",
