      "The **Sea Woman** is the most prominent non-human person in Inuit cosmology, though her name, origin story, and attributes vary by region. In Igloolik and surrounding areas, she is Takannaaluk ('the one down there'); among Kivalliq Inuit, Nuliajuk ('dear little wife'); in parts of Greenland, Arnakuagsak or Sassuma Arnaa ('Mother of the Deep'). The most widely known narrative describes her as a young woman whose father, during a storm or famine, threw her overboard from a boat to save himself; as she clung to the gunwale, he cut off her fingers, which became seals, walruses, and whales. She sank to the sea floor, where she rules over marine mammals, controlling their availability to hunters. When humans violate taboos, the Sea Woman's hair becomes tangled and dirty (representing the pollution of human transgressions), and she withholds animals. Shamans must journey to her underwater realm, comb her hair, and confess community violations to persuade her to release animals again.",
